  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAn amplified -PNA upper patterns exists across the CONUS \nthrough the weekend, which is resulting in mild fall \ntemperatures and dry conditions for northern MI. \n\nCurrent satellite and surface observations depict widespread dense \nfog over much of the Great Lakes region this morning. The 00z RAOB \nshows an ideal radiational cooling profile, with surface charts \nindicating that a surface high pressure continues to settle in to \nthe east of the state this morning. Light east winds just above the \nnocturnal surface inversion are drifting low stratus and fog to the \nwest, continuing the spread inland and covering most of northern MI. \nWith the trends of this fog and stratus, there will likely be enough \naround to slow the lifting and clearing of it after the sun\nrises. Eventually, southeast winds and late morning sunshine \nshould lift and scattered fog and low clouds out - which is \nexpected to be by noon for most of northern lower and eastern \nupper. Some low clouds could hang right along and/or just off \nshore of the coast into the early afternoon, however overall \nvisibilities should improve by noon. Light south to southeast \nwinds and mostly sunny skies will be seen this afternoon. High \ntemperatures will warm into the mid 60s for NW lower, and low \n60s for NE lower, with high 50s for eastern upper. NE lower \ncould see slightly warmer temperatures if clouds dissipate \nquicker than expected. \n\nTonight, similar conditions (ideal radiational cooling, light winds, \nand the warm lakes/residual moisture in the environment) will led to \nanother round of fog and low stratus. A wind shift to the south and \nincreasing mid to high level clouds over NW lower should keep \nwidespread dense fog confined to NE lower and eastern upper. \n\nSaturday, the persisting upper ridge over the eastern CONUS (and MI) \nwill track precipitation chances up and over it, into CAN. Mid to \nhigh clouds will be seen at times with light winds and continued \nmild temperatures. A cold front will approach early Sunday, brining \nslight chances for showers which are favored in the U.P. at this \ntime due to the boundary weakening as it moves across the state to \nthe south and east. However, a 1035mb surface high pressure \nwill seep into the area Monday, cooling temperatures for early \nnext week. High temperatures will likely remain in the 40s and \n50s for Monday and Tuesday over most of northern MI. Slight \nchances for light rain exist Monday and Tuesday as a shortwave \nraces towards MI (on top of a beaten down upper high). Ensembles\nshow little agreement on the timing of this feature next week, \nleading to overall lower precipitation chances spread out early\nweek through mid week.",
